The section under review is a two-line change that adds `except exception.DIDFilterSyntaxError: raise` before the broad `except Exception` clause. This is a pure error-handling change: it re-raises a specific exception so it is not swallowed and converted into `DataIdentifierNotFound`. It does not build, concatenate, or execute any SQL, and it does not introduce any new dynamic input into the SQL text.

The SQL construction itself (in `create_postgres_query` and the `sql.SQL(...).format(...)` in `list_dids`) is pre-existing, unchanged code. The values are escaped via `value.replace("'", "''")` and keys are validated against `^[A-Za-z0-9_]+$`, and the table name is wrapped in `sql.Identifier`. Any injection concern there would be in unchanged code, which per the rules is not reportable.

The changed lines only affect which exception propagates to the REST handler (which maps `DIDFilterSyntaxError` to HTTP 400). This does not create or expose a SQL injection.

